body:
Currently, when creating a table with a long name and long column names, the ability to use the `->foreignId(...)->constrained()` method goes away, because the auto-generated constraint name is too long. The current workaround for this is to use the non fluent builder method (see below), but becomes cumbersome to have to do. 

```php
 Schema::create('my_execptionally_long_table_name', function (Blueprint $table) {

            // This will fail because the constraint name is too long
            $table->foreignId('my_super_long_column_name')->constrained('my_other_really_long_table_name');

            // this is the current workaround to the issue
            $table->foreignId('my_super_long_column_name')
            $table->foreign('my_super_long_column_name', 'my_custom_short_constraint_name')->references('id')->on('my_other_really_long_table_name');

            // Proposed alternative short syntax
            $table->foreignId('my_super_long_column_name')->constrained('my_other_really_long_table_name', 'id', 'my_custom_short_constraint_name');
        });
```

This PR adds the constraint name as an argument to the `->constrained` function call, so that a custom name can be specified for the constraint instead. 

Ideally, the ability to chain a `->name('constraint_name`) on the `->constrained` call would be a more ideal solution, but that seemed non-trivial given how the current constrained helper is working. If that is also the preferred final solution, then  I can put some more time in to that.

@greenhat 

Will add the rest of the `Constant` methods in next PRs, just want to check that you're happy with the direction I'm going.

Allowing more flexibility in how `Constant`s are created as opposed to specific constructor methods like `Constant.from_coll_coll_byte` like we have at the moment. `constants.test.ts` tests should show examples of before vs after
